      "paperId": "4538f74b4b22f42e907664715cf30006454f86ef",
      "title": "Revisiting Schools of Cartographic Thought: The Soviet School and Marxism-Leninism",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Olesia Ignateva",
          "authorId": "2185071466"
        },
        {
          "name": "Stanislav Ronzhin",
          "authorId": "2363259521"
        },
        {
          "name": "M. Kraak",
          "authorId": "153323475"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "R\u00c9SUM\u00c9:Dans la seconde moiti\u00e9 du XXe si\u00e8cle, l\u2019\u00e9cole sovi\u00e9tique de pens\u00e9e cartographique a propos\u00e9 un cadre th\u00e9orique alternatif en cartographie. Konstantin Salichtchev, fondateur de cette \u00e9cole et acteur de premier plan de l\u2019Association cartographique internationale, s\u2019opposait au paradigme de la communication, largement accept\u00e9 par les chercheurs occidentaux. Cette opposition s\u2019expliquait par l\u2019adoption g\u00e9n\u00e9ralis\u00e9e de l\u2019id\u00e9ologie marxiste-l\u00e9niniste par les g\u00e9ographes et cartographes sovi\u00e9tiques. Le concept de la \u00ab carte comme mod\u00e8le de la r\u00e9alit\u00e9 \u00bb constituait une id\u00e9e centrale de l\u2019\u00e9cole sovi\u00e9tique. Il s\u2019alignait \u00e0 la fois sur la th\u00e9orie du reflet de L\u00e9nine et sur les exigences pratiques de production massive de cartes. Ayant pour fondement le mat\u00e9rialisme dialectique, le marxisme-l\u00e9ninisme imposait le principe du non-r\u00e9ductionnisme \u00e0 la science sovi\u00e9tique, ce qui a conduit au rejet du paradigme de la communication cartographique. L\u2019analyse des actes des conf\u00e9rences cartographiques internationales de 1976 et 1980 r\u00e9v\u00e8le la sous-repr\u00e9sentation des recherches centr\u00e9es sur l\u2019utilisateur au sein de l\u2019\u00e9cole sovi\u00e9tique. Bien que plusieurs autres th\u00e9oriciens de la cartographie se soient oppos\u00e9s \u00e0 Salichtchev (dissidents cartographiques), ils n\u2019ont pas transform\u00e9 l\u2019\u00e9cole de pens\u00e9e. Les successeurs de Salichtchev ont \u00ab h\u00e9rit\u00e9 \u00bb de cette \u00e9cole, en appliquant \u00e9galement un paradigme cognitif aux sciences de l\u2019information g\u00e9ographique et \u00e0 la t\u00e9l\u00e9d\u00e9tection. En raison de l\u2019absence de recherches sur la communication cartographique, l\u2019adoption du paradigme de la g\u00e9ovisualisation semble difficile dans la Russie contemporaine.ABSTRACT:In the second half of the twentieth century, the Soviet school of cartographic thought offered an alternative theoretical framework for cartography. Konstantin Salishchev, the school\u2019s founder, was a prominent leader of the International Cartographic Association who debated against the communication paradigm widely accepted by Western scholars. The reason was the widespread adoption of the Marxism-Leninism ideology by Soviet geographers and cartographers. The concept of \u201cmap as a model of reality\u201d was a key idea of the Soviet school. It was aligned with Lenin\u2019s reflection theory and the practical demand for high-quantity map production. Having dialectic materialism at its core, Marxism-Leninism imposed the principle of nonreductionism on Soviet science, which led to the rejection of the cartographic communication paradigm. An analysis of the proceedings of the International Cartographic Conference of 1976 and 1980 brought evidence of the underrepresentation of user study research within the Soviet school. Although several other theoreticians of cartography opposed Salishchev (cartographic dissidents), they did not change the school of thought. The successors of Salishchev \u201cinherited\u201d the school, also applying a cognitive paradigm to geoinformation science and remote sensing. Due to the lack of cartographic communication research, adopting the geovisualization paradigm seems challenging in contemporary Russia.",
